    <h1>Motivation Blog</h1>
    <div class = "content-box">
        <p>
            I recently read Atomic Habits by James Clear and he explains that true behavior change isn't actually about what you want to achieve. Infact it is about who you wish to become. When we try to start a new routine, most of us focus entirely on the end goal. However, real change happens when you shift your focus from outcome-based goals to indentity-based tracking. How successful a person is in changing their habits has a lot to do with the mindset they are bringing into their effort of changing habits. </p>
            
        <p>
            This is why instead of telling yourself that you need to grind through an intense workout to hit a target weight, reframe the habit. You need to change your mindset from trying to achieve a goal to simply proving to yourself that you are a person who doesn't miss a workout. Every time you open your dashboard and log a healthy habit, you are essentially casting a vote for that new identity. Building disclipine and setting up a good routine is not as much about forcing yourself to have endless willpower as it is about setting up a system where you make good choices naturally. 
        </p>
        <p>
            James Clear mentions that every habit follows a neurological feedback loop that consists of four simple steps: cue, craving, response, and reward. In order to make a habit stick, you have to optimize this loop by making the action "obvious", "attractive", "easy", and "satisfying". For example, if you want to start drinking more water, put your water bottle right in the center of your desk, so you can't miss it. This will naturally help to remind you that you need to drink water every time you look at the water bottle. By changing the structure of your environment to make healthy habits the path of least resistance, you take the pressure off of your willpower. 
        </p>
        <p>
            James Clear goes on to explain that one of the most frustrating parts of starting any new wellness of lifestyle journey is the lack of immediate results. For example, you work out for two weeks or eat clean for 10 days, and then look at yourself in the mirror expecting some changes. However, looking back at you from the mirror is the same person that you started with, nothing has changed. James Clear calls this phase the "Plateau of Latent Potential". It is easy to get discouraged and quit because we expect linear progress. However, in reality small, daily choices don't show their true value until weeks or months of consistent repetitions finally break through. This is why it is important to have the mindset that you are not trying to achieve a goal in a day or a couple of days. You are just trying to become a 1% better version of yourself every day.  
        </p>

